<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter-10" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Divergence, Curl, and Surface Integrals</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    In this chapter, we study divergence and curl, two fundamental quantities that describe how vector
    fields behave locally. Divergence measures sources and sinks, while curl captures rotational
    behavior. Together, these ideas provide insight into how a vector field spreads out or circulates
    at each point in space.
  </p>

  <p>
    We begin by interpreting divergence and curl conceptually before focusing on computation.
    Understanding what these quantities mean geometrically helps clarify their role in fluid flow,
    electromagnetism, and other applications. We then connect these ideas to conservative vector
    fields and earlier concepts from line integrals and Green’s Theorem.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chapter continues with surface integrals, extending integration to curved surfaces in space.
    Both scalar and vector surface integrals are introduced, with careful attention to orientation and
    geometric interpretation. By the end of the chapter, you should be able to set up and evaluate
    surface integrals and understand how divergence and curl influence flux across surfaces.
  </p>
</introduction>

